      "id": "past-eat-object",
      "pattern": "[Time word] + [Past verb] + الـ[Object, accusative]",
      "exampleArabic": "أمس أكل جدي التفاح",
      "exampleTransliteration": "amsi akala jaddī t-tuffāḥa",
      "exampleEnglish": "Yesterday grandfather ate the apple.",
      "grammarNote": "أكل's past tense is fully regular — unlike its present tense (آكُلُ's hamza merger), the past tense reads exactly like ذَهَبْتُ, ذَهَبَ, ذَهَبْنَا with a different root: أَكَلْتُ، أَكَلَ، أَكَلْنَا. The 'I/you/you/she' collision from level 22 applies here too: أَكَلْتُ، أَكَلْتَ، أَكَلْتِ، and أَكَلَتْ all collapse to أكلت without diacritics. جَدِّي ('my grandfather') applies the possessive ي- ending from level 9 to a brand-new noun — the grammar was already yours, only the word is new.",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_amsi_akala_jaddi_altuffaah", "text": "أمس أكل جدي التفاح" },
      "introducedInLevel": 28
    }
  ],
